# Moderation

- Moderation never writes another plugin's data. An action on a reported target is a form in the Reports panel that posts to the owning plugin's own route (`annotations/<id>/hide/`, `submissions/close/`). That route checks `Origin`, its own permission with `can()`, writes its own audit entry, and redirects to a validated same-site `returnTo`. Moderation shows the form only when the viewer holds that permission, only when the plugin is on the vault (`ctx.plugins` at build time), and only when that route would take this target; `can()` throws for a permission no plugin declared. Revert is a link instead, to history's diff page for the commit (`?to=<sha>#sv-history-revert`), where history's own form is: reverting has one code path. The panel finds the pages a commit changed with submissions' `changedFiles` and checks `history:reverts:write` on them with one `canEach`.
- Every action stays inside its vault, even where vaults share a repository or a database. Close takes only a pull request submissions' sync recorded saving for this vault (`isVaultSubmission`), and the panel asks the same before offering it.
- Reading another plugin's data goes through an export of its server entry (`findComment`/`findComments` from `@svartz/plugin-annotations/server`, `isVaultSubmission`/`savedEdits` from `@svartz/plugin-submissions/server`), with the binding name moderation's build read from that plugin's options.
- The Reports panel reads everything its rows need in list lookups (`lookUp` in `panel.ts`: `findComments`, `savedEdits`, auth's `findProfilesBy*`, `findProfiles`, `canEach`), never one per report: a free Worker gets 50 D1 calls a request, and `tests/panel-cost.test.ts` holds a full page to the cost of a handful. A lookup that fails leaves only the rows that need it undescribed or without their actions.
- An edit names the account to suspend only through submissions' record of saving it (`savedEdits`), never its author, committer or `Svartz-Account` trailer. Never query another plugin's tables directly. Moderation's `emitArtifacts` runs `enforce: "post"`, so it can read submissions' build data (the vault's folder in the repository).
- Resolving a report and acting on its target stay separate actions, each audited.
- An action that changes rows commits them in one batch with its audit entry, so its data lives in `SVARTZ_DB`. Put `auditIfChanged` right after a guarded statement (`INSERT … SELECT … ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`), so a target gone or already changed records nothing. When the plugin keeps its data elsewhere, its route answers 503 `misconfigured` and the panel leaves the action off; never write the change and the entry to two databases.
- A `returnTo` that passes as a same-site path can still normalize into another site: `/a/..//evil.test/` becomes `//evil.test/`. Redirect to the raw value or, after normalizing, refuse a path starting with `//`.
- Every reportable thing is a button marked `data-svartz-report="<kind>:<id>"` with `data-svartz-report-label`; moderation's browser script answers it by delegation. A plugin adds Report to its own views this way, without importing moderation.
- Look up a key a reader chose (a target id, a query parameter, a stored report's target) only in a `Map` or behind `Object.hasOwn`. Build data arrives as plain objects, where `constructor`, `toString` and `__proto__` answer for every key. The panel renders a report it can't describe as a plain row, so one bad row never takes the queue down.
- The panel never renders who reported. Every reader-supplied string (reason, comment body, names) goes through `escape`.
- User pages live where auth says (`userPageLocation()` on the server, auth's `userPages` option at build time); never hardcode `users/`.
- Reports of commits and submissions are validated by form only (SHA, number) because moderation doesn't call GitHub; the owning route checks the rest when a moderator acts.
